why: service-lifecycle gave a process a drain (readiness off, delay,
  in-flight finish) but no rendered manifest tells its platform to
  WAIT for it. Kubernetes kills at 30 s by default and compose at 10,
  which is under the 2 + 15 the lifecycle asks for; a drain nobody
  waits for is a drain that does not happen.

what: Health gains stopSeconds (default 30, above the lifecycle's
  2 + 15). Rendered where the target has a documented equivalent:
  Kubernetes terminationGracePeriodSeconds, compose stop_grace_period,
  systemd TimeoutStopSec, ECS stopTimeout if it is one line. NO
  preStop hook: the process handles SIGTERM itself (okay-ops Signals).
  Regenerate the committed renderings; the drift tests hold them.

